{"name":"Rhino Tusk","hex":"#d4a278","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rhino","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Money. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rhinoceros."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rhino"},{"term":"tusk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of a pair of elongated pointed teeth (either cuspids or incisors) that extend outside the mouth of an animal such as walrus, elephant or wild boar, and which continue to grow throughout the animal's life.","A small projection on a (tusk) tenon."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To dig up using a tusk, as boars do.","To gore with the tusks."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tusk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rhinoceros","blocks":[{"text":"A rhinoceros, commonly abbreviated to rhino, is a member of any of the five extant species of odd-toed ungulates (perissodactyls) in the family Rhinocerotidae. It is the only living family in the superfamily Rhinocerotoidea. Two of the extant species, the black rhinoceros and the white rhinoceros are native to Africa, while the other three, the Javan rhinoceros, Indian rhinoceros and Sumatran rhinoceros are native to South and Southeast Asia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/081_White_rhinoceros_%28male%29_in_the_Kalahari_Desert_of_Namibia_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/330px-081_White_rhinoceros_%28male%29_in_the_Kalahari_Desert_of_Namibia_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhinoceros"},{"title":"Tusk","blocks":[{"text":"Tusks are elongated, continuously growing front teeth that protrude well beyond the mouth of certain mammal species. Most commonly known as canine teeth, as with narwhals, chevrotains, musk deer, water deer, muntjac, pigs, peccaries, hippopotamuses and walruses, or, in the case of elephants and hyrax, elongated 